#JMeter Learning and Practical Implementation – Tata 1mg

1. Overview

As part of the monthly KRA, this activity focuses on learning Apache JMeter and understanding the fundamentals of performance testing.

Tata 1mg is used as a reference web application to gain practical experience in creating and configuring a basic JMeter test plan.

The learning includes understanding JMeter components, configuring HTTP requests, managing virtual users, handling test data using CSV files and understanding how performance test results are displayed.

 2. Learning Objectives

- Understand the purpose of performance testing.
- Learn Apache JMeter installation and its interface.
- Understand the structure of a JMeter Test Plan.
- Learn Thread Groups and virtual user configuration.
- Understand HTTP Request Samplers and HTTP Request Defaults.
- Learn how to configure request headers and response assertions.
- Understand CSV-based test data handling.
- Explore Listeners and performance result reports.
- Understand basic performance testing concepts and metrics.
- Develop a reusable JMeter test setup for different applications.

3. Tool Used

**Apache JMeter**

Apache JMeter is an open-source tool used to perform load and performance testing of applications and services.

It helps simulate user requests and analyse how a system responds under different workloads.

4. Topics Learned and Practical Implementation

4.1 JMeter Installation and Interface

- Understood the purpose of Apache JMeter.
- Explored the JMeter graphical user interface.
- Understood the basic structure and navigation of JMeter.

4.2 Test Plan

A Test Plan is the main container that holds all the components required to configure a test.

Practical implementation:
- Created a Test Plan named Tata1mg Performance Test.
- Saved the test plan in JMeter `.jmx` format.

4.3 Thread Group

A Thread Group controls the virtual users and execution settings.

Important configurations:

| Setting | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Number of Threads | Defines the number of virtual users |
| Ramp-up Period | Defines the time taken to start virtual users |
| Loop Count | Defines how many times each user executes the test flow |

Practical configuration:
- Number of Threads: 1
- Ramp-up Period: 1 second
- Loop Count: 1

4.4 HTTP Request Sampler

An HTTP Request Sampler defines the request that JMeter sends to the target application.

Practical implementation:
- Created an HTTP Request Sampler named Tata1mg Homepage Request.
- Configured the HTTP method as GET.
- Configured the request path as `/`.
- Used HTTPS and `www.1mg.com` through HTTP Request Defaults.

4.5 HTTP Request Defaults

HTTP Request Defaults allows common request settings to be configured in one place.

Practical implementation:
- Added HTTP Request Defaults under the Thread Group.
- Configured the protocol as HTTPS.
- Configured the server name as `www.1mg.com`.
- Cleared the protocol and server name from the individual HTTP Request Sampler so that it inherits these settings.

 4.6 HTTP Header Manager

HTTP Header Manager is used to configure headers that are sent along with HTTP requests.

Practical implementation:
- Added HTTP Header Manager under the HTTP Request Sampler.
- Configured the `Accept` header.
- Set its value to `text/html`.

4.7 Response Assertion

Response Assertions are used to validate whether an HTTP response meets the expected condition.

Practical implementation:
- Added a Response Assertion under the HTTP Request Sampler.
- Selected Response Code as the field to test.
- Configured the matching rule as Equals.
- Set the expected response code to `200`.

4.8 CSV Data Set Config

CSV Data Set Config allows JMeter to read test data from an external CSV file and use it through variables.

Practical implementation:
- Added CSV Data Set Config under the Thread Group.
- Created a CSV file named `search_data.csv`.
- Configured the variable name as `Search_Product`.
- Configured the first line to be ignored because it contains the column header.
- Added sample product data.

Sample CSV data:

```csv
Search_Product
Paracetamol
Cetirizine
Dolo 650
```

The variable `${Search_Product}` can be used in HTTP requests to read the corresponding product value from the CSV file.

4.9 Listeners

Listeners are used to view and analyse test execution results.

The following Listeners were added:

**View Results Tree**
- Displays individual request and response details.
- Helps inspect response codes and response data.

**Summary Report**
- Displays overall request statistics.
- Includes average, minimum and maximum response times, throughput and error percentage.

**Aggregate Report**
- Provides aggregated performance statistics.
- Helps analyse response time, throughput, request failures and percentiles.

5. Basic Performance Testing Concepts

**Load Testing**

Evaluates application behaviour under an expected user load.

**Stress Testing**

Evaluates application behaviour when the workload increases beyond its expected capacity.

**Spike Testing**

Evaluates application behaviour when traffic increases suddenly.

**Endurance Testing**

Evaluates application stability under sustained load over a longer period.

6. Important Performance Metrics

| Metric | Description |
|---|---|
| Response Time | Time taken by the application to respond |
| Throughput | Number of requests processed over a period |
| Error Percentage | Percentage of failed requests |
| Average Response Time | Average time taken to process requests |
| 90th Percentile | Time within which 90% of requests complete |
| 95th Percentile | Time within which 95% of requests complete |

